[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Cache Eviction Policies: LRU, LFU, FIFO, and ARC"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern system design, memory is finite. When caches (such as Redis, Memcached, or CPU caches) reach maximum capacity, they must evict existing items to accommodate new data. Choosing the right cache eviction policy is critical for optimizing hit ratios, reducing latency, and managing infrastructure costs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. First-In, First-Out (FIFO)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"FIFO is the simplest eviction policy. It operates like a queue: the oldest entry in the cache is the first one to be evicted when capacity is reached, regardless of how frequently or recently it has been accessed."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Extremely easy to implement using a simple queue data structure with O(1) time complexity."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Suffers from Belady's anomaly and poor hit rates because it ignores access patterns. Frequently accessed items get evicted just because they were inserted early."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Least Recently Used (LRU)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"LRU evicts the items that have not been accessed for the longest period of time. It relies on the principle of temporal locality: if an item was accessed recently, it is likely to be accessed again soon."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implementation: Typically implemented using a Hash Map combined with a Doubly Linked List to achieve O(1) operations for both lookups and updates."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Vulnerable to cache pollution during batch operations or full table scans, where a flood of infrequently used items flushes out hot data."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"class LRUCache {
    constructor(capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.map = new Map();
    }
    get(key) {
        if (!this.map.has(key)) return -1;
        const val = this.map.get(key);
        this.map.delete(key);
        this.map.set(key, val);
        return val;
    }
    put(key, value) {
        if (this.map.has(key)) this.map.delete(key);
        else if (this.map.size >= this.capacity) {
            const firstKey = this.map.keys().next().value;
            this.map.delete(firstKey);
        }
        this.map.set(key, value);
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Least Frequently Used (LFU)"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"LFU tracks how often an item is accessed. The item with the lowest access count is evicted first. If there is a tie, a secondary policy (like LRU) is typically used to break it."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Excellent for workloads with stable, long-term popularity skew where hot items remain hot."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Struggles with shifting access patterns. An item that was heavily accessed in the past may linger in the cache forever even if it is no longer needed, unless a decay mechanism is implemented."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Adaptive Replacement Cache (ARC)"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"ARC is a self-tuning, patent-free algorithm that balances between LRU (recency) and LFU (frequency). It dynamically adjusts its internal parameters based on workload characteristics to achieve superior hit rates compared to standard LRU."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mechanism: Maintains two lists, one tracking recency and the other tracking frequency, alongside ghost lists that track recently evicted items to measure what would have happened if cache sizes were different."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Adapts automatically to changing workloads without manual tuning."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary Comparison for Architects"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"FIFO: Use when simplicity is paramount and access distribution is uniform."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LRU: The industry standard general-purpose cache policy (used in Redis maxmemory-policy volatile-lru)."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LFU: Ideal for heavy read scenarios with skewed, predictable popularity curves."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ARC: Best-in-class hit rates for complex, mixed, or highly dynamic workloads, albeit with slightly higher computational overhead."}]
